### What is Recursion?

Recursion is a technique where a method solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller version of the same problem. Every correct recursive method has two parts: a base case that returns a direct answer without recursing and a recursive case that reduces the problem and calls the method again. Each call gets its own stack frame on the call stack, and the frames unwind as the base case is reached.

At National Bank of India, Rajesh uses recursion to walk a chain of linked savings accounts and compute the combined balance.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountChain {
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private AccountChain next;

    public AccountChain(BigDecimal balance, AccountChain next) {
        this.balance = balance;
        this.next = next;
    }

    public static BigDecimal totalBalance(AccountChain account) {
        // Base case: end of chain
        if (account == null) {
            return BigDecimal.ZERO;
        }
        // Recursive case: this balance + total of the rest
        return account.balance.add(totalBalance(account.next));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AccountChain chain = new AccountChain(new BigDecimal("50000.00"),
            new AccountChain(new BigDecimal("75000.00"),
                new AccountChain(new BigDecimal("30000.00"), null)));
        System.out.println("Total balance: " + totalBalance(chain));
    }
}
```

```text
Total balance: 155000.00
```

### Why Does Recursion Exist?

Recursion exists because many problems are naturally self-similar. A linked list is a node followed by a smaller linked list. A tree is a root connected to smaller trees. A sorted array can be halved into two smaller sorted arrays. When the structure of the data is recursive, writing a recursive solution maps directly to the structure and produces cleaner code than an iterative version.

At National Bank of India, the organizational hierarchy is a tree where each branch manager oversees smaller teams. Computing the total headcount under any manager is natural with recursion.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class OrgNode {
    String name;
    List<OrgNode> reports = new ArrayList<>();

    public OrgNode(String name) { this.name = name; }

    public static int headcount(OrgNode manager) {
        if (manager == null) return 0;
        int count = 1; // count the manager
        for (OrgNode report : manager.reports) {
            count += headcount(report);
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

### How Does Recursion Work Internally?

Every time a method calls itself, the JVM pushes a new frame onto the call stack. That frame stores the local variables, the parameters, and the address to return to when the call finishes. As the recursion goes deeper, more frames pile up. When the base case finally returns, the frames pop off one by one and each caller receives the result from its callee.

The call stack for `totalBalance` with three accounts looks like this during execution:

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class StackDemo {
    public static BigDecimal totalBalance(String label, BigDecimal balance, int depth) {
        String indent = "  ".repeat(depth);
        System.out.println(indent + "PUSH frame: " + label + " balance=" + balance);
        if (balance == null) {
            System.out.println(indent + "BASE CASE: return 0");
            return BigDecimal.ZERO;
        }
        // Simulate next account
        BigDecimal rest = (depth < 2)
            ? totalBalance("Account" + (depth + 2), new BigDecimal("10000"), depth + 1)
            : totalBalance("null", null, depth + 1);
        BigDecimal result = balance.add(rest);
        System.out.println(indent + "POP frame: " + label + " returns " + result);
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        totalBalance("Account1", new BigDecimal("50000"), 0);
    }
}
```

```text
PUSH frame: Account1 balance=50000
  PUSH frame: Account2 balance=10000
    PUSH frame: Account3 balance=10000
      PUSH frame: null balance=null
      BASE CASE: return 0
    POP frame: Account3 returns 10000
  POP frame: Account2 returns 20000
POP frame: Account1 returns 70000
```

### How Is Recursion Different from Iteration?

Recursion and iteration can both solve repetitive problems, but they differ in mechanism and suitability.

| Aspect | Recursion | Iteration |
|---|---|---|
| Mechanism | Method calls itself | Loop repeats a block |
| State storage | Each call gets a stack frame | Variables in the loop body |
| Memory | O(n) stack frames for depth n | O(1) extra memory |
| Overflow risk | StackOverflowError on deep input | No overflow risk |
| Code clarity | Matches self-similar structures | Better for flat sequences |
| JVM optimization | No tail-call optimization | Naturally efficient |

Here is the same account-chain total written iteratively:

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class IterativeTotal {
    public static BigDecimal totalBalance(AccountChain account) {
        BigDecimal sum = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        AccountChain current = account;
        while (current != null) {
            sum = sum.add(current.getBalance());
            current = current.getNext();
        }
        return sum;
    }
}
```

### What Are the Rules for Writing Correct Recursion?

Every recursive method must follow three rules to work correctly.

First, define a base case that returns without recursing. Without it the method calls itself forever. Second, ensure the recursive case reduces the problem toward the base case on every call. If the input does not shrink, the base case is never reached. Third, do not modify shared mutable state between calls unless you explicitly manage it, because each frame sees its own locals but shares heap objects.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class InterestCalculator {
    // Computes compound interest for n periods recursively
    public static BigDecimal compoundInterest(BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal rate, int periods) {
        // Rule 1: base case
        if (periods == 0) {
            return principal;
        }
        // Rule 2: reduce toward base case (periods - 1)
        BigDecimal grown = principal.multiply(BigDecimal.ONE.add(rate));
        return compoundInterest(grown, rate, periods - 1);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal result = compoundInterest(
            new BigDecimal("100000"), new BigDecimal("0.08"), 5);
        System.out.println("Amount after 5 years: " + result.setScale(2, java.math.RoundingMode.HALF_UP));
    }
}
```

```text
Amount after 5 years: 146932.81
```

### What Exceptions Can Occur?

The most common exception in recursion is `StackOverflowError`. It occurs when the recursion goes too deep and the call stack runs out of space. The default stack size in the JVM is typically 512 KB to 1 MB, which allows roughly a few thousand frames.

Here is code that triggers the error:

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class StackOverflowDemo {
    // Missing proper base case: will overflow
    public static int badSum(int n) {
        return n + badSum(n - 1); // no base case!
    }

    // Correct version with base case
    public static int goodSum(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) return 0;  // base case stops recursion
        return n + goodSum(n - 1);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            badSum(10);
        } catch (StackOverflowError e) {
            System.out.println("StackOverflowError: recursion never stops");
        }
        System.out.println("Good sum of 10: " + goodSum(10));
    }
}
```

```text
StackOverflowError: recursion never stops
Good sum of 10: 55
```

### What Are Common Mistakes?

The first common mistake is forgetting the base case entirely. The second is writing a base case that the recursive case never reaches. The third is not reducing the problem, so the same input repeats forever.

Wrong code where the problem does not shrink:

```java
// WRONG: passes the same list, never shrinks
public static BigDecimal totalWrong(List<BigDecimal> balances, int index) {
    if (index >= balances.size()) return BigDecimal.ZERO;
    return balances.get(index).add(totalWrong(balances, index)); // BUG: index not incremented
}
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: index increments toward the base case
public static BigDecimal totalCorrect(List<BigDecimal> balances, int index) {
    if (index >= balances.size()) return BigDecimal.ZERO;
    return balances.get(index).add(totalCorrect(balances, index + 1)); // index + 1 shrinks problem
}
```

### What About Tail Recursion and JVM Optimization?

Tail recursion is when the recursive call is the very last operation in the method, meaning no work is done after the call returns. Some languages like Scala and Kotlin on the JVM optimize tail recursion into a loop so the stack does not grow. The standard Java compiler and JVM do not perform this optimization.

This means that for deep recursion problems in Java, you should convert the recursion to an explicit loop or use an explicit stack data structure.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class ExplicitStackTotal {
    // Using an explicit stack instead of recursion
    public static BigDecimal totalWithStack(AccountChain head) {
        Deque<AccountChain> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        AccountChain current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            stack.push(current);
            current = current.getNext();
        }
        BigDecimal sum = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            sum = sum.add(stack.pop().getBalance());
        }
        return sum;
    }
}
```

### When Should You Use Recursion?

Use recursion when the data structure is naturally self-similar: trees, linked lists, graphs, and divide-and-conquer problems. Use iteration when the problem is a flat sequence, when the depth could be very large, or when performance and memory matter more than code clarity.

At National Bank of India, Rajesh uses recursion for traversing the organizational tree, for walking nested JSON in API responses, and for divide-and-conquer sorting of transaction batches. He uses iteration for scanning flat lists of transactions, computing running balances, and processing sequential queue items.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class RecursionGuideline {
    // Good use of recursion: tree traversal
    public static void printOrgTree(OrgNode node, int depth) {
        if (node == null) return;
        System.out.println("  ".repeat(depth) + node.name);
        for (OrgNode child : node.reports) {
            printOrgTree(child, depth + 1);
        }
    }

    // Bad use of recursion: flat list sum (use a loop instead)
    public static long sumList(List<Long> amounts, int i) {
        if (i >= amounts.size()) return 0;
        return amounts.get(i) + sumList(amounts, i + 1);
    }
}
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly follow up with these questions after discussing recursion:

- Convert a recursive solution to iterative. This tests whether you understand that the call stack can be replaced by an explicit stack or loop.
- Identify the time and space complexity of a recursive method. The space is at least O(depth) because of the stack frames.
- Explain memoization. This is caching the results of recursive calls to avoid recomputing overlapping subproblems.
- Write a recursive solution for factorial, Fibonacci, or binary search. These are the standard warm-up problems.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class RecursionClassics {
    // Factorial: base case is 0! = 1
    public static long factorial(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return 1;
        return n * factorial(n - 1);
    }

    // Fibonacci with memoization
    public static long fib(int n, long[] memo) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        if (memo[n] != 0) return memo[n];
        memo[n] = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);
        return memo[n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("5! = " + factorial(5));
        System.out.println("fib(10) = " + fib(10, new long[11]));
    }
}
```

```text
5! = 120
fib(10) = 55
```
